<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Romanos III Argyros</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">968–1034</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Romanos III</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Romanos III Argyros was Byzantine emperor from 1028 to 1034, a senior aristocrat who came to the throne by marrying Zoe, daughter of Constantine VIII. In 1030 he led an army against the Mirdasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> and was heavily defeated near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azaz">Azaz</a>, though his general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-maniakes">George Maniakes</a> later took Edessa. His reign was marked by heavy spending on churches, and he died in his bath in 1034, possibly murdered.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/romanos-iii-argyros.jpg" alt="Romanos III Argyros" width="187" height="242" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown, 13th-century author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Romanos_III_in_Madrid_Skylitzes.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
